    <section class="use-cases-grid technical-grid" aria-label="Duo Input technical details">
        <article class="use-cases-panel">
            <h2>Deployment fit</h2>
            <details open>
                <summary>No internet required</summary>
                <p>Duo Input can operate without an internet connection, which supports controlled environments and dedicated installations. Because the input handling is local to the system, the shared-screen workflow can remain responsive without depending on a cloud roundtrip for every interaction. This is useful for service desks, counters and fixed customer terminals where predictable behavior matters.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Installer size</summary>
                <p>The installer is about 50-150 MB. This makes it practical for managed rollout in dedicated environments where the target hardware, displays and workflow are known in advance.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Installed footprint</summary>
                <p>Disk space after installation is about 100-250 MB, depending on configuration. The footprint is intended to stay small enough for fixed service setups, embedded-style installations and customer-facing workstations.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Runtime memory usage</summary>
                <p>Memory usage during runtime is about 50-150 MB. This helps keep the system suitable for always-on customer-facing workflows where staff need the service screen to remain responsive while the customer interacts on a second display.</p>
            </details>
        </article>

        <article class="use-cases-panel">
            <h2>Platform coverage</h2>
            <details open>
                <summary>Windows 10</summary>
                <p>Supports Windows 10 in both 32-bit and 64-bit environments. This can be relevant for businesses with existing installed hardware where replacement cycles are slow and customer-facing systems need to keep working across older workstation setups.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Windows 11</summary>
                <p>Supports Windows 11 in 64-bit environments. This makes Duo Input suitable for newer service workstations and modern multi-display deployments where a staff screen and customer screen are connected to the same system.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Linux</summary>
                <p>Supports selected Linux distributions for embedded and dedicated setups. This can support controlled physical environments where the business wants a stable, purpose-built installation for kiosks, counters or assisted-service terminals. The same principle can support touchscreens, external displays and customer-facing hardware used for input, confirmation and signing.</p>
            </details>
        </article>
    </section>
